 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "cyprus_venetian_walls",
   "title": "The Venetian Walls",
   "text": "The Venetians built these walls in the 1560s, a great star with eleven bastions, to keep the Ottomans out. In 1570 the Ottoman army took the city anyway. The walls still ring the old town, about three miles round, and the dry moat is now car parks, gardens and a very good place for cats."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "cyprus_famagusta_gate",
   "title": "Famagusta Gate",
   "text": "The Famagusta Gate was the main way into the walled city from the east, on the road to Famagusta. Its long vaulted passage through the rampart is now a cultural centre: concerts and exhibitions where the guards once checked the carts."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "cyprus_nicosia",
   "title": "One City, Three Names",
   "text": "Nicosia is Lefkosia in Greek and Lefkoşa in Turkish, and it is the capital of the Republic of Cyprus. A line has run through the middle of it since the 1960s, so it is often called the last divided capital in Europe. Walk to the top of Ledra Street and you'll see why. Both halves keep the same clock, Cyprus time, though the north followed Turkey's UTC+3 all year from September 2016 until late 2017."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "cyprus_british_rule",
   "title": "British Cyprus, 1878 to 1960",
   "text": "Britain took over the running of Cyprus from the Ottoman Empire in 1878, annexed it in 1914 and made it a Crown colony in 1925. From 1955 to 1959 EOKA, a Greek Cypriot movement for union with Greece (enosis), fought British rule, while Turkish Cypriot leaders called for partition (taksim). British, Greek Cypriot and Turkish Cypriot lives were lost. Cyprus became independent on 16 August 1960, and Britain kept two Sovereign Base Areas, about three per cent of the island."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 4,
   "plaque": "cyprus_green_line",
   "title": "The Green Line",
   "text": "At Christmas 1963 fighting broke out between Greek Cypriots and Turkish Cypriots in Nicosia. To stop it, the story goes, a British officer drew a ceasefire line across a map of the city in green pencil. UN peacekeepers came in 1964. The pencil line became sandbags, then oil drums and walls, and it is still here."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "cyprus_1974",
   "title": "Summer 1974",
   "text": "In July 1974 a coup backed by the military government in Athens overthrew President Makarios, aiming at union with Greece. Five days later Turkey's army landed in the north. Greek Cypriots remember it as the invasion; many Turkish Cypriots remember it as an intervention that ended years of fear. By August the island was split. Well over a hundred thousand Greek Cypriots fled south and tens of thousands of Turkish Cypriots moved north, and thousands of people on both sides were killed or never found."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "cyprus_ledra",
   "title": "Ledra Street",
   "text": "Ledra Street, the old city's main shopping street, runs straight up to the buffer zone and stopped dead there for decades. In April 2008 a crossing opened at the top, and now people walk through with a passport or ID card to shop, eat, visit family or just look. The first crossings on the island opened in 2003."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "cyprus_halloumi",
   "title": "Halloumi",
   "text": "Halloumi is a firm, salty cheese of sheep's and goat's milk (and, these days, often cow's), cooked in its own hot whey and folded with mint. It grills without melting and squeaks on the teeth. In 2021 the EU registered it as a protected product of Cyprus under both its names: halloumi in Greek, hellim in Turkish."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "cyprus_euro",
   "title": "The Euro and the Mouflon",
   "text": "Cyprus joined the European Union in 2004 and swapped the Cypriot pound for the euro in 2008. Your sterling won't buy a coffee here. Look at the small coins: the 1, 2 and 5 cent pieces carry the mouflon, the island's wild sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "cyprus_annan",
   "title": "The 2004 Vote",
   "text": "In April 2004 both communities voted on a UN plan to reunite the island, the Annan Plan. About two thirds of Turkish Cypriots voted yes and about three quarters of Greek Cypriots voted no, so it fell. A week later the whole island joined the EU, but EU law is on hold in the north until there's a settlement. Talks have come and gone ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "OLD NICOSIA. LEDRA STREET and the crossing, north. PYLA and DHEKELIA, south."
  }
 ],
